Durarara!! AU
Chapter 4 – Part 4
Shinra tied off the final suture on the head wound and stepped back, stripping off his gloves.
Curtis immediately tested the edge of the chair like a man preparing to stand.
“Okay. Now are we finished?”
The question was hopeful in the most exhausted way possible—like if he asked politely enough, the three people who had already blocked him once might suddenly decide he was free to walk out, find a clean shirt, and go to the bar he had mentioned earlier.
Shinra gave him a long, assessing look.
“Medically? The holes are closed. You’re no longer actively pouring blood onto my floor.” A small pause. “In every other sense? No. Not even close.”
Shizuo crossed his arms.
“You’re staying until that head wound stops looking like it’s going to open again if you sneeze. And until someone makes sure you’re not going to wander off and pass out in an alley.”
Celty typed a short message and held the PDA up.
Rest first.
Then we talk about where you live.
Curtis leaned back in the chair, long black braids settling against the freshly stitched scalp, and let out a slow breath through his nose. The expression on his face said he was already calculating how long he would have to sit there before they stopped watching him long enough to leave anyway.
Curtis stayed seated, but his mismatched eyes shifted to Celty’s yellow helmet and the PDA still in her gloved hand.
“Hmm. Sure.” A short pause. “But first—tell me why you keep talking with that phone.”
The question was calm, almost casual, but it cut straight to the obvious oddity in the room.
Celty’s helmet tilted slightly. For a moment she didn’t move. Then she typed a careful response and turned the screen toward him.
I cannot speak the normal way.
This is how I communicate.
Shinra’s expression brightened immediately, the earlier seriousness giving way to his usual enthusiasm at the chance to explain.
“Ah! Yes—Celty doesn’t have a voice in the conventional sense. The PDA is her primary method of conversation. Quite elegant, really, once you get used to reading along.”
Shizuo grunted from the wall.
“You get used to it or you don’t. She still gets her point across.”
Celty added a second line on the PDA, the tone a little dryer this time.
You already knew I used it.
Why ask now?
Curtis glanced at the screen, then at the dark visor of the helmet, and gave the smallest shrug—like the answer was complicated and he wasn’t in the mood to fully unpack it in front of Shinra and Shizuo.
The fact that he could hear her real voice remained, for the moment, unsaid.
Curtis stood up.
Before anyone could stop him, he closed the short distance to Celty, reached up with both hands, and lifted the yellow cat-eared helmet straight off her shoulders.
There was no head beneath it.
Only the clean, unnatural absence where a head should have been—the dark riding suit ending at the neck in a way that defied ordinary biology. The empty space somehow still carried presence, still felt directed toward him.
Curtis looked at it for a moment, completely unsurprised.
“Ah. That checks out.” His voice stayed even, almost thoughtful. “That’s why I couldn’t see a face behind the visor.”
He wasn’t unnerved.
He wasn’t shocked.
He simply stood there holding the yellow helmet like he had confirmed a minor theory, long black braids hanging still against his bloodied shirt.
The apartment froze.
Shinra’s mouth opened, then closed, eyes wide behind his glasses—not with fear, but with the sudden realization that this foreigner had just done something most people in Ikebukuro would never dare.
Shizuo straightened fully, the usual aggression replaced by sharp, wary attention. “Oi—!”
Celty’s posture had gone rigid. One gloved hand lifted halfway, then stopped. The empty space where her head should have been remained fixed on Curtis, the silence heavier than any message on a PDA could have been.
Curtis glanced from the helmet in his hands back to the headless figure in front of him, still entirely unbothered by what he was looking at.
Curtis looked at the empty space above Celty’s shoulders for another second, then calmly set the yellow helmet back into place. He adjusted it once so it sat correctly, the same way someone might straighten a friend’s collar, and stepped back.
Then he shrugged, long black braids shifting against the bloodied shirt.
“What? Why are you all rigid?”
The question was genuine. He looked between Shinra, Shizuo, and the now re-helmeted Celty like their tension was the strange part of the interaction, not the fact that he had just casually removed a Dullahan’s head covering and confirmed she had no head.
Shinra’s voice came out higher than usual.
“Because most people—when confronted with a headless woman—tend to scream, run, or at the very least react! You put the helmet back on like you were returning a borrowed hat!”
Shizuo stared at him, the earlier wariness deepening into something closer to disbelief.
“You’re not even phased. Not even a little. What the hell are you?”
Celty stood completely still. One gloved hand lifted toward the helmet Curtis had just replaced, then lowered again. After a moment she typed a slow message and turned the PDA toward him.
You’re the first person who has ever taken it off without asking.
And the first who didn’t look afraid.
The words sat in the quiet apartment with more weight than the earlier revelations about scars or blood types. Curtis had treated the most defining, isolating truth of her existence like a minor detail that simply “checked out.”
He glanced at the screen, then at the three of them still watching him like he had grown a second head of his own, and looked mildly confused by the continued tension.
Curtis’s brow furrowed slightly, the expression more puzzled than annoyed.
“If a headless woman makes you all scared, then you would all shit yourselves in the forest where I grew up.”
The statement landed with the same flat certainty he had used for everything else.
Shinra’s mouth opened, closed, then opened again. The scientific excitement from earlier was now mixed with something closer to genuine unease.
“…What exactly was in that forest?”
Shizuo’s eyes narrowed. The sense of familiarity he had felt toward Curtis twisted into something heavier. People who talked about headless women like they were a mild inconvenience usually had their own category of wrongness—and the foreigner’s scars, blood type, and survival history were already painting a picture Shizuo didn’t like.
Celty’s helmet remained fixed on Curtis. She typed slower than before, the message carrying a different weight this time.
What did you see there?
Curtis glanced at the PDA, then at the three of them, and seemed to realize—again—that he had said more than he meant to. The closed-off look tried to settle back into place, but the implication of his words already hung in the apartment: whatever had lived in those woods had made a Dullahan look ordinary by comparison.
He didn’t answer right away.
Curtis leaned back in the chair slightly, long black braids shifting as he finally gave them more than a single-word answer.
“It’s Appalachia. Cryptids. Fae. Spirits. Demons. All sorts of stuff.” His tone stayed matter-of-fact, like he was listing local wildlife. “Most of them just try to kill you. But some are really nice—contrary to their urban legends. Like the Mothmen.”
The apartment absorbed that in silence for a full second.
Shinra’s eyes had gone wide behind his glasses, the scientific hunger roaring back to life with a vengeance.
“Mothmen. Plural. You’re telling me the Appalachian region has a stable enough population of cryptids and fae that you can categorize their temperaments—and that some of the entities commonly associated with death omens are, in your experience, friendly?”
Shizuo stared at Curtis like the foreigner had just confirmed every bad feeling he’d been having since the street fight.
“You’re saying you grew up around things that make her—” he jerked his chin toward Celty “—look normal.”
Celty’s helmet remained very still. After a moment she typed a careful message and turned the PDA toward Curtis.
You survived that.
Alone.
It wasn’t framed as a question. The weight behind the words was clear.
Curtis glanced at the screen, then at the three of them, and gave a small, almost dismissive shrug—like surviving a forest full of things that wanted him dead was simply what had happened, not something that required further discussion.
“Yeah,” he said. “I did.”
Curtis looked at the yellow helmet with the same calm he had shown since removing it earlier.
“To be honest, Celty—by the way, nice to meet you—you’re like the third headless woman I’ve met.”
The casual delivery made the statement land even harder.
In his mind, the thought followed immediately, dry and practical:
But where’s her head? Don’t these type of fae carry their heads at their side?
He didn’t say that part out loud. He simply studied the space above her shoulders for a moment longer, as if the absence of a visible head under one arm was the real puzzle.
Shinra made a sound that was half gasp, half delighted choke.
“The third? You’ve met multiple Dullahan-class entities and you’re ranking them by order of introduction?!”
Shizuo’s expression had gone flat with the particular kind of exhaustion that came from learning the foreigner was somehow even less normal than previously established.
“Of course he has. Why wouldn’t he.”
Celty stood very still. For several seconds she didn’t type. When she finally did, the message on the PDA was slower, more careful than usual.
Most people only ever meet one.
If they meet any at all.
You have met three.
A second line appeared after a brief pause.
And you are wondering where my head is.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes flicked to the screen. He didn’t confirm or deny the thought she had somehow read in his silence. He simply held her gaze through the dark visor, waiting.
Celty’s real voice came quietly, meant for only one person in the room.
“I bet he’s wondering where my head is. Just who is this foreigner…”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes shifted to the yellow helmet. He tilted his head slightly, long black braids moving with the motion, and answered like the conversation had been happening out loud the entire time.
“See, that’s what I mean. Why use the phone to talk if you can?”
The apartment went dead silent.
Shinra and Shizuo both turned to stare at him.
From their perspective, Celty had not typed anything. The PDA screen was still showing the previous message. No artificial voice. No new text. Just the headless rider standing still—and the bloodied foreigner responding to something neither of them had heard.
Shinra’s voice came out careful, almost delicate.
“…Curtis. Who are you talking to right now?”
Shizuo’s eyes narrowed dangerously.
“She didn’t say anything. The phone didn’t say anything. So what the hell did you just answer?”
Celty’s posture had gone rigid again. The yellow helmet remained fixed on Curtis, the weight of the accidental reveal settling heavily between them. She had spoken freely, assuming the usual rules still applied—and he had answered her in front of the two people who were never supposed to know he could hear her.
Curtis glanced between the three of them, seeming to realize a half-second too late that he had just made the private channel public.
Curtis’s brows furrowed hard.
The combined stares from Shinra and Shizuo, the tension radiating off Celty, the weight of everything he had already given away in one afternoon—it all stacked too high.
“Okay. Now you’re all just fucking with me.”
He stood up in one sharp motion, long black braids swinging against the bloodied shirt as he stepped around Celty and toward the door.
“I’m leaving.”
“Curtis—” Shinra started.
Shizuo moved to block him. “Oi, sit your ass—”
Curtis didn’t stop. He shoved past the blond man’s shoulder with enough force to make the point without turning it into another fight, pulled the door open, and stepped out into the hallway. The ruined white shirt, the fresh stitches, the still-seeping head wound, the scars, the blood type, the forest, the headless women—none of it was enough to make him stay and explain himself further.
The door shut harder than necessary behind him.
For a moment the apartment was left in stunned quiet.
Celty’s helmet slowly turned toward the closed door. Shinra took off his glasses and rubbed his face. Shizuo stared at the spot where the foreigner had been, the sense of familiarity now tangled with irritation and something closer to reluctant concern.
Curtis was already halfway down the stairs, mismatched eyes fixed forward, putting distance between himself and the people who had seen too much of him in too short a time.
The door had already closed.
Shinra was saying something. Shizuo was cursing under his breath. The apartment still carried the residual tension of everything that had just been revealed and then abruptly abandoned.
Celty barely registered any of it.
She stood in the same place Curtis had left her, yellow helmet angled toward the door, the PDA hanging forgotten at her side. The empty space where her head should have been felt heavier than usual.
He can hear me.
The thought turned over slowly, carefully, like something fragile that might break if she examined it too quickly.
Not the PDA.
Not the artificial voice.
Not text on a screen that people had to look at to understand.
He had heard her.
The real voice she had not used in front of anyone for longer than she could cleanly measure. The one that should have been impossible for a human—or anything close to human—to perceive. And he had answered it without hesitation, without fear, without even seeming to find it strange.
Then he had walked out before any of them could demand an explanation.
Celty’s gloved fingers curled slowly at her side.
He can hear me.
And now he was gone.
The door stayed shut.
For a few seconds the only sounds in the apartment were the faint drip of leftover blood on the floor and Shinra’s slightly elevated breathing.
Shinra put his glasses back on, eyes still wide with the particular gleam he got when something refused to fit into ordinary categories.
“He answered her,” Shinra said quietly, almost to himself. Then louder, turning to Shizuo. “You saw that. Celty didn’t type. The PDA didn’t speak. And he responded like she had just spoken out loud. That is not a coincidence. That is not a lucky guess. That man can hear her.”
Shizuo’s scowl deepened. He was still staring at the door Curtis had used.
“Yeah. I noticed.” His voice was low, rough. “Same way I noticed he took a full trash can, a board to the skull, two stab wounds, and still walked out of here complaining about his shirt. Same way I noticed the scars. And the blood type. And the forest full of things that make a Dullahan look normal.”
He finally looked at Shinra.
“You’re about to ask a hundred questions the second you see him again, aren’t you.”
Shinra didn’t even try to deny it. His smile returned, sharper and more focused than usual.
“Of course I am. Rh-null blood. Repeated near-fatal blood loss with spontaneous recovery. Scar tissue that suggests contact with both mundane and non-mundane trauma. Multiple confirmed encounters with headless entities. And the ability to perceive Celty’s actual voice.” He adjusted his glasses, practically vibrating. “Do you have any idea how many research papers that combination could generate? How many established models of human limitation it breaks?”
Celty’s helmet turned slowly from the door toward the two of them. She lifted the PDA and typed a short message, holding it so both could read.
He left because we pushed.
If you corner him again, he will disappear.
Shinra’s enthusiasm dimmed only slightly.
“I’m not going to corner him. I’m going to invite him. Politely. With medical follow-up as the excuse. And then I’m going to ask very carefully about the Appalachian forest, the other headless women, and exactly how he is able to hear you.”
Shizuo let out a short, humorless breath.
“You’re going to get punched. Or worse.”
“Possibly.” Shinra’s smile didn’t fade. “But it will be worth the data.”
Celty lowered the PDA. The empty space beneath the yellow helmet remained directed at the door, the same quiet thought from earlier still turning over in the silence she usually lived with.
He can hear me.
And now two of the people she trusted most in the city knew it too.
Shinra was still talking when Celty moved.
She stepped over to the chair Curtis had been sitting in and paused. Something small and slightly crumpled had been left on the seat—overlooked in the rush of his exit. She picked it up carefully between gloved fingers and turned it over.
A photograph.
Two boys, both young, smiling so wide it looked like the picture could barely contain it. One was unmistakably a younger Curtis—same mismatched eyes, same long dark hair that would later be braided, the same sharp structure to his face even before the years of scars. The other boy was unfamiliar: lighter skin, brighter expression, an arm thrown around Curtis’s shoulders like they had never known a day apart.
Celty flipped the photo over.
On the back, written in slightly faded ink:
dear curtis
im sorry i couldnt carry on,
i hope you forgive me
- justice
She stood still for a long moment, then held the photograph out so Shinra and Shizuo could see both sides.
Shinra’s earlier excitement cooled into something quieter and more complicated as he read the message.
“…Justice,” he murmured. “A name. And a goodbye.”
Shizuo’s expression tightened. He didn’t reach for the photo, but his eyes stayed fixed on the smiling younger version of the man who had just walked out bleeding and closed-off.
“Kid looks happy there,” he said after a second. “Both of them do. Whatever happened after that picture… it wasn’t good.”
Celty typed slowly, the message carrying more weight than usual.
He left this behind.
On purpose, or by accident?
Neither Shinra nor Shizuo had an answer.
The photograph sat between them—proof that the foreigner who treated headless women as ordinary and near-death as routine had once been a boy smiling next to someone named Justice, and that someone had not been able to keep going.
The apartment felt heavier than it had a minute ago.